{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB13365 - MONDO_1566",
  "verbalization": "Calcium Lactate Gluconate is associated with Hypercalcemia Disease through a series of indirect pathways, primarily through its role in phosphorus metabolism. The drug is linked to Phosphorus Metabolism Disease, which in turn is associated with Calcium Acetate, a compound that directly contributes to Hypercalcemia Disease. This suggests a potential mechanism where the metabolic disturbances caused by Phosphorus Metabolism Disease may influence calcium homeostasis, leading to hypercalcemia. \n\nAdditionally, Calcium Lactate Gluconate is also linked to Nephrocalcinosis, a condition that indirectly contributes to Hypercalcemia Disease via the accumulation of calcium in the kidneys. While the exact mechanism linking Nephrocalcinosis to hypercalcemia remains unclear, it highlights the drug's broader impact on calcium-related pathophysiology.\n\nThe evidence suggests that Calcium Lactate Gluconate may contribute to Hypercalcemia Disease through its involvement in phosphorus metabolism and kidney function, though the precise causal pathway remains to be fully elucidated. The integration of these pathways underscores the drug's potential role in the development of hypercalcemia, particularly in patients with underlying metabolic disturbances.",
